A||N||2||Taijiguy||Rear end alignment situation- Help!||||1||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||04-29-2001||04:21 PM||||OK, I just dropped the cradle and replaced the flywheel. In the process I discovered the control arm bushings were horribly loose. So I replaced those, and just got it back on all fours. Now I see that the toe is <B>WAY</B> off. I mean, the right is turned out in the front by a good inch, the back, well, hard to tell since the camber is so far off. Is this likely the result of the new bushings? I can hardly believe that the old ones, however bad could have caused this much variation in the alignment. <BR>Any thoughts or suggestions? I did notice that after replacing the bushings, the sway bar end link on the right didn't want to go back on, was a bear to line up.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark||04-29-2001||08:49 PM||||Small changes in rear camber make big changes in rear toe.  If you changed bushings, this would be a good time to check everything (ball joints, struts, tie rod ends), then get a 4-wheel alignment.||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||04-30-2001||09:20 AM||||Absolutly! Check it all out. I recently did a transplant on my 84 Cradle and all and the rear was way off. after I centerd the steering wheel I ran fishing line from the front fender across the center of the (just touching the sidewall front and rear)front wheel and to the rear wheel. I adjusted the rear so that the front and rear tire wall just touch the line. This gave me (assuming the front was in decent shape) a close enough to get by setup so that at least the car was not going down the street sideways!||||reg||3
